  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "About the Greek alphabet",
      "body": "<p>Greek has been written with this alphabet for about 2,800 years. The Greeks took the letters from the Phoenicians and made one big change: they used some letters for vowels. That made it the first alphabet with full vowels, and the parent of Latin, Cyrillic, Coptic and Gothic.</p>\n<p>Mathematicians and scientists use the letters too, so you may already know π, Σ, Δ, λ and Ω.</p>\n<p>This app teaches Modern Greek sounds. Ancient Greek was said differently (β was b, η was a long e), which is why English words from Greek do not always match.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "The stress mark",
      "body": "<p>Every Greek word of two or more syllables carries an accent (<span lang=\"el\">ά, έ, ή, ί, ό, ύ, ώ</span>) on its stressed vowel. It is part of the spelling and it can change the meaning:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"el\">πότε</td><td>póte, when?</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"el\">ποτέ</td><td>poté, never</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Two dots (<span lang=\"el\">ϊ, ϋ</span>) mean the vowel is said on its own, not as part of a pair: <span lang=\"el\">ρολόι</span> is ro-ló-i, clock.</p>\n<p>Capital letters at the start of a word keep the accent at their upper left (<span lang=\"el\">Ώρα</span>); text in all capitals drops it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Five ways to spell ee",
      "body": "<p>The sound ee can be written <span lang=\"el\">ι, η, υ, ει</span> or <span lang=\"el\">οι</span>, and o can be <span lang=\"el\">ο</span> or <span lang=\"el\">ω</span>. Each spelling was once a different sound in Ancient Greek; they merged over the centuries, but spelling kept the old forms.</p>\n<p>For reading this is good news: each of these always says ee. For writing it means learning which spelling a word uses. Grammar helps: many endings always use the same one, like <span lang=\"el\">-η</span> for feminine nouns (<span lang=\"el\">η αγάπη</span>, love) and <span lang=\"el\">-ω</span> for the first person of verbs (<span lang=\"el\">γράφω</span>, I write).</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Final sigma",
      "body": "<p>Small sigma has two shapes. Inside a word it is <span lang=\"el\">σ</span>; at the end of a word it is <span lang=\"el\">ς</span>:</p>\n<p lang=\"el\">σεισμός</p>\n<p>seismós, earthquake: the first two sigmas are σ, the last is ς. The capital is always Σ.</p>"
    }
  ]
